= Choosing our enemies

In our media and campaign materials, who should we consider as our main opponents? 

Currently, the LPS remains our biggest threat. Ross Thatcher's Liberals have presented themselves as the free-enterprise alternative to our party. 

[? if enemies != 0 : Previously, we had considered the [+ enemies +] as our prior enemies. Is a change of direction necessary? ?]

- @anti_lps: We need to focus our attacks on the Liberals.
- @anti_scps: Social Credit is the biggest threat to this campaign.
- @anti_establishment: We must take on the establishment: the PCPS and the LPS.
- @anti_pcps: We need to go after the PCPS.
- @easy_discard: Let's drop this and move on.



@anti_scps
on-arrival: enemies = "SCPS"; left_hardliner_strength += 5; left_hardliner_dissent -= 5; workers_scps -= 3; workers_scps -= 3;
subtitle: [? if year >= 1963 : The momentum of the SCPS has halted; their supporters seem to be drifting to either the Liberals or Tories ?]
view-if: year >= 1935

Social Credit is a strong populist movement, more amenable to the economic conditions of farmer-dominated Saskatchewan in some aspects than us. It seems a wise choice to target them. 

@anti_lps
subtitle: The dominant party right now
on-arrival: enemies = "LPS"; scps_urgency += 1; workers_lps -= 2; farmers_lps -= 2; new_middle_lps -= 2;

The LPS are our biggest opponents right now, 

@anti_pcps
on-arrival: enemies = "PCPS"; pcps_relation -= 5

The party would earn much from distancing itself from the electorally-poisionous Communists

@anti_establishment
on-arrival: enemies = "establishment parties"; left_hardliner_strength += 8; left_hardliner_dissent -= 5; pcps_relation -= 5; pps_relation -= 5; cpc_s_relation += 5; managerial_dissent += 8; moderate_dissent += 8; workers_pps -= 3; coalition_dissent += 1

Targetting the old-line parties earns us populist goodwill.